     We provide a shim layer between the cciissss interface and CAM(4), offloading
     most of the queueing and being-a-disk chores onto CAM.  Entry to the
     driver is via the PCI bus attachment cciissss__pprroobbee(), cciissss__aattttaacchh(), etc.
     and via the CAM interface cciissss__ccaamm__aaccttiioonn(), and cciissss__ccaamm__ppoollll().  The
     Compaq cciissss adapters require faked responses to get reasonable behavior
     out of them.  In addition, the cciissss command set is by no means adequate
     to support the functionality of a RAID controller, and thus the supported
     Compaq adapters utilize portions of the control protocol from earlier
     Compaq adapter families.

     Currently cciissss supports the “simple” and “performant” transport layer.

     Non-disk devices (such as internal DATs and devices attached to the ex‐
     ternal SCSI bus) are supported as normal CAM devices provided that they
     are exported by the controller firmware and are not marked as being
     masked.  Masked devices can be exposed by setting the
